    "outputs": {
      "DIFFICULTY_EXPLANATION": "The engineering difficulty comes from making ten different Linux object families share normal Terraform lifecycle semantics without hiding state in a second store: identity must remain stable across mutable changes, refresh must observe native external state, mutations must be transactional from Terraform's point of view, and resource-specific ownership rules must converge safely under retries and drift.",
      "README_PATH": "terraform-ansible-managed-resources/README.md",
      "SOLUTION_EXPLANATION": "The reference implementation centralizes lifecycle identity and normalization, uses Ansible only for Create/Update/Delete mutations, uses native Linux observation for Read/refresh, publishes Terraform state only after successful mutation, preserves prior managed state on failed update/delete, and uses structured process execution with timeout/cancellation handling and temporary-playbook cleanup. Resource implementations retain their documented filesystem, content, text, account, group and cron ownership semantics.",
      "VERIFICATION_EXPLANATION": "The verifier maps the public requirement families to 30 failure-to-pass and 6 preservation tests covering schemas, identity, native drift, transactional failure/retry, scoped deletion, content/text/account/cron behavior, ownership collisions, runner safety, normalization and the Ansible mutation backend. SPEC_ALIGNMENT independently resolved Q1 as NO_GAP, Q2 as COVERED and Q3 as CLEAR. Runtime/oracle empirical success is not claimed here and remains for the later deterministic validation stage."
    },
